---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Connect an MCP client"
  description="Let a host such as Claude Code discover Clink tools, read prompt shortcuts, and make a call over a local stdio process."
  toc={[
    ["register", "Register the server"],
    ["discover", "Discover and call tools"],
    ["prompts", "Prompt shortcuts"],
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="register">Register the server</h2>
  <p>
    For Claude Code follow the{" "}
    <a href="/quick-start/#claude">project-local registration</a>. For a host
    that accepts a JSON stdio configuration, replace the two absolute paths
    below. With uv, <code>uv tool dir --bin</code> prints the directory
    containing the executable.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="MCP client configuration"
    code={JSON.stringify(
      {
        mcpServers: {
          clink: {
            command: "/absolute/path/to/clink-mcp-server",
            args: ["--project", "/absolute/path/to/your-project"],
          },
        },
      },
      null,
      2,
    )}
  />
  <p>
    The client launches the server and owns stdin and stdout. Restart or
    reconnect the client after configuration changes. Each server process binds
    one project. See <a href="/configuration/#project">project setup</a> to
    generate manifests and portable role prompts.
  </p>
  <h2 id="discover">Discover and call tools</h2>
  <p>
    Use <code>tools/list</code> to get each tool’s name and JSON input schema.
    Pass the chosen name and arguments to <code>tools/call</code>. The following
    script uses the MCP Python SDK that ships as a dependency of Clink. Run it
    with the Python from a Clink installation and put{" "}
    <code>clink-mcp-server</code> on PATH.
  </p>
  <Code
    label="Python · local discovery"
    code={
      'import asyncio\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\n\nasync def main():\n    params = StdioServerParameters(\n        command="clink-mcp-server", args=["--project", str(Path.cwd().resolve())]\n    )\n    async with stdio_client(params) as (reader, writer):\n        async with ClientSession(reader, writer) as session:\n            await session.initialize()\n            tools = await session.list_tools()\n            print([tool.name for tool in tools.tools])\n            result = await session.call_tool("version", {})\n            print(result.content[0].text)\n\nasyncio.run(main())'
    }
  />
  <p>
    Clink normally returns JSON as the text of an MCP content block. Check the
    inner status and error fields as well as any protocol-level error.{" "}
    <a href="/mcp/tools/">Browse the 18 tools and their arguments</a>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="prompts">Prompt shortcuts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>prompts/list</code> advertises a shortcut for each enabled tool and a{" "}
    <code>continue</code> prompt. <code>prompts/get</code> returns the messages
    for the selected shortcut. The host decides how to display them. For
    example, the <code>review</code> prompt guides the host toward{" "}
    <code>codereview</code>, and <code>thinkdeeper</code> maps to{" "}
    <code>thinkdeep</code>. Calling a tool directly uses its tool name.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stdio server advertises tools and prompts. Attach file context through
    tool arguments using approved local paths.
  </p>
</Docs>
